# Team records, tracked deliberately as OTHER PEOPLE'S WORK — several by operators who
# have since left the branch. `5c4bc461` force-added them "verbatim and unedited", and
# `.ai/.../F3-baseline-predictions.md` is the visual-regression gate's own instrument:
# a prediction is only worth anything if it is provably the one that was registered
# before the run.
#
# Prettier must not touch them. `pnpm format` was observed rewriting the registry
# during the format-debt sweep, which would have committed a formatter's edits to
# another operator's record under this operator's name — the same silent-attribution
# problem as the two commit incidents, arriving through a tool nobody suspects.
#
# An entry rather than a habit because the habit has to hold for every operator on
# every run, and the cost of one miss is a rewritten record that reads as authored.
.ai

# `@ts-expect-error` is LINE-SCOPED, so line breaks in this file are load-bearing.
# Every directive in it is an assertion in both directions (the file says so at its
# head): it fails the build if its line stops erroring, so it cannot rot into a
# comment. Prettier re-wraps the single-line JSX into a multi-line element, which
# moves the offending prop several lines away from the directive above it — one
# assertion broke loudly (`TS2322` on `selection={{ mode: 'multiple' }}`), and the
# rest are one re-wrap away from silently suppressing a DIFFERENT error than the one
# they name, which no test would catch.
#
# Same class as the golden fixtures above: the tool's output is correct and the
# file's meaning still depends on its layout.
packages/ui-react/src/components/ui/data-grid/__tests__/props-identity-rule.types.test.tsx
